## 8. Matrix, Status Pixels, and Visual Effects

The WS2812B matrix is the selected primary display and remains exactly 256
pixels. General AAC-CE architecture continues to allow approximately 3–4
discrete WS2812 status LEDs on a separate chain and data GPIO; they are never
appended to the matrix. Dad's current 8x32 build instead reserves two pixels
in the panel's bottom-right status area and does not plan three external
status LEDs. The selected panel driver owns GP8 when compiled in.

For Dad's build, logical pixel (31,7), the bottom-right pixel, is reserved for
combined timing/GPS/PPS/satellite health. It is very dim green when GPS and PPS
are valid/locked and satellite count is greater than 4; very dim amber when
GPS and PPS are valid/locked with exactly 4 satellites; and very dim red when
GPS is invalid, PPS is invalid/not locked, or satellite count is below 4. Four
satellites is the minimum acceptable count: amber marks that minimum margin,
green marks healthier margin, and red means part of the required input chain
has failed (often an antenna-placement issue in ordinary use). This is a quiet,
unobtrusive status indicator, not an alert.

Pixel (31,6), directly above the health pixel, is reserved for holdover state.
It is off normally and, only while the clock is actually operating in
HOLDOVER, is intended to breathe slowly with a soft pinkish/muted red at low
maximum brightness. The rise and fall should be smooth, without flashing,
abrupt alerts, or harsh alarm red. Its exact RGB, minimum/maximum brightness,
and period remain open for later physical tuning. The two pixels have distinct
meanings: live input health at (31,7), and active holdover at (31,6); therefore
red health can coexist with a softly breathing holdover indication.

These real-state indicators are intended as final or near-final compositor
overlays. Future backgrounds, inverted displays, holiday/birthday scenes, and
decorative effects must not overwrite them. Only (31,7) is on the bottom row;
(31,6) belongs to the same bottom-right status area, and the rest of row 7
remains available for other future uses. Other WS2812 builds may adopt this
on-panel pattern when appropriate. No status-pixel behavior is implemented.
The physical panel has visibly responded to the raw-index chase. Logical
mapping is implemented as top-left origin, left-to-right column progression,
and alternating vertical traversal, matching the physically observed panel
topology. Raw chase, RGB transport, corners, perimeter, rows, columns, the
temporary 5x7 font, and the full-height 6x8 candidate were physically
evaluated. The 6x8 candidate was judged too large. The approved clock-specific
variable-width 5x7 glyph table is implemented, pending physical verification.
The former fills,
