Future scroll, dissolve, trails, glow, particles, swirl, and holiday
transitions consume old and new logical positions rather than assuming a
permanent x coordinate. They remain unimplemented and must preserve clear
time-quality indication.

### Clock glyph shape and paint semantics

The selected clock font defines a geometric glyph mask. The renderer or
compositor paints that shape using a logical RGB clock paint value; the glyph
data is independent of its color. Ordinary colors and black/off are all valid
paint values, including amber, red, green, blue, white, and black/off. In
particular, RGB(0, 0, 0) is an intentional clock paint, not an error or a
request for a separate inverse font.

Black/off paint explicitly writes RGB(0, 0, 0) at glyph pixels, forcing those
pixels dark. Transparency is different: a transparent/no-op pixel leaves the
existing underlying pixel unchanged. The compositor must preserve this
distinction. For example, a future illuminated background/effect can remain
visible around the glyph mask while black/off clock paint makes the time
readable as dark cutouts. Later overlay layers may add content such as a
day-of-month marker, status mark, or holiday accent.

The implemented minimal logical compositor uses this order:

    background/effect layer
        -> clock glyph mask painted with logical RGB clock paint
        -> overlay layer(s)
        -> logical framebuffer
        -> output processing
        -> display transport

The static presentation palette contains soft white RGB(255, 240, 220), white
RGB(255, 255, 255), reference amber RGB(255, 96, 0), and red, green, blue,
cyan, and magenta. The everyday default is soft white at brightness 2; amber
remains the alternate/reference at brightness 6. Development WS2812 proof
commands are `p norm`, `p amber`, `p invert`, and `p bg`. They select black
background/soft-white clock, black background/amber clock, soft-white
background/black glyphs, and blue background/soft-white clock respectively.
These are static proofs only. Later scenes and effects build on the same
logical composition boundary. Transfer correction stays downstream and
independent; production remains transfer off.

Keep the font geometric and logical. It does not own colors, foreground or
background policy, effects, holidays, themes, brightness, transfer correction,
current limiting, or display transport. Compositing and output stages consume
logical RGB; black/off remains RGB(0, 0, 0) through global brightness, future
transfer correction and current protection, and becomes black pixel data at
WS2812 serialization. Do not add transport-specific blackout encoding or a
special inverse-mode flag.

The minimal compositor and black/off versus transparent semantics are
implemented in the renderer/compositor boundary. Keep this foundation small
before adding substantial theme, holiday, or effect work.

Animation style is not fixed. Fades, dissolves, decays, color transitions, and
rolling effects are candidates for early development-build testing. Fonts,
overlays, calendar information, and special-day themes may be explored in
development builds. Production effects, status hierarchy, and exact layouts
remain to be specified and must preserve clear time-quality indication.

Transfer characterization is now available in the WS2812 development build.
`g off` selects the current identity output and preserves current behavior;
`g g2` selects a generic inverse-gamma-2 comparison; `g ws` selects an
illustrative WS2812-aware low-code comparison. Neither candidate is approved
for production. `status` reports the selected transfer. The generic curve is
`round(sqrt(code * 255))`; this is inverse gamma 2, which lifts low codes
(31→89) and explains why it can look too bright. It is the opposite direction
from squaring encoded values to linearize them. The WS comparison uses a continuous ease-out,
`code + round(code * (255 - code) / 255)`, which expands low codes and tapers
to identity at full scale. It is motivated by reported WS2812-family
low-code behavior and is not characterized for this BTF panel. No LUT is used.

Use `pat amber` for the reference amber at brightness 6; `pat r`, `pat g`,
`pat b`, `pat w`, `pat c`, and `pat m` show primary/secondary colors. `pat off`
returns to the clock. `ramp r`, `ramp g`, `ramp b`, and `ramp gray` show
pre-transfer codes 0..31 across the 32 columns at serializer brightness 255.
Each x column is exactly `RGB(x,0,0)`, `RGB(0,x,0)`, `RGB(0,0,x)`, or
`RGB(x,x,x)` for all eight rows, then goes through the selected transfer once.
These controls clear and fully own the framebuffer; they are development-only
and return to normal clock rendering without pausing timekeeping.

User-reported physical bench observations on the BTF panel through the current
